Given, \[\vec{A}=xy\,\hat{a}_{x}+x^{2}\,\hat{a}_{y}\] The closed line integral is evaluated using Green's theorem: \[\oint_{C} \vec{A}\cdot d\vec{l}
=
\oint_{C} \left(A_{x}\,dx+A_{y}\,dy\right)
=
\iint_{S}
\left(
\frac{\partial A_{y}}{\partial x}
-
\frac{\partial A_{x}}{\partial y}
\right)\,dx\,dy\] Here, \(A_{x}=xy, A_{y}=x^{2}\) Therefore, \[\frac{\partial A_{y}}{\partial x}=2x,
\qquad
\frac{\partial A_{x}}{\partial y}=x\] Hence, \[\frac{\partial A_{y}}{\partial x}
-
\frac{\partial A_{x}}{\partial y}
=
2x-x=x\] <img src="img/f7c9fa178603c05f.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> The rectangular region is bounded by \[\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} \le x \le \frac{2}{\sqrt{3}},
\qquad
1 \le y \le 3\] Thus, \[\oint_{C} \vec{A}\cdot d\vec{l}
=
\int_{1/\sqrt{3}}^{2/\sqrt{3}}
\int_{1}^{3}
x\,dy\,dx\] \[=
\int_{1/\sqrt{3}}^{2/\sqrt{3}}
2x\,dx\] \[=
\left[x^{2}\right]_{1/\sqrt{3}}^{2/\sqrt{3}}
=
\frac{4}{3}-\frac{1}{3}
=
1\] \[\boxed{\text{(c) 1}}\]

The given logic circuit consists of a \(4 \times 1\) Multiplexer (MUX) with selection lines \(S_1 = A\) and \(S_0 = B\). The output equation of a standard \(4 \times 1\) MUX is given by: \[F = \overline{S_1}\,\overline{S_0}I_0 + \overline{S_1}S_0I_1 + S_1\overline{S_0}I_2 + S_1S_0I_3\] From the given circuit diagram, the input lines are connected as follows: <ul><li>\(I_0 = C\)</li><li>\(I_1 = D\)</li><li>\(I_2 = \overline{C}\) (via NOT gate)</li><li>\(I_3 = \overline{C}\,\overline{D}\) (via bubbled AND gate)</li></ul> Substituting the selection lines and inputs into the MUX equation: \[F = \overline{A}\,\overline{B}C + \overline{A}BD + A\overline{B}\,\overline{C} + AB(\overline{C}\,\overline{D})\] To find the minterms, we expand each term into its standard canonical Sum-of-Products (SOP) form: \[\begin{aligned}
    \overline{A}\,\overline{B}C &amp; = \overline{A}\,\overline{B}C(D + \overline{D}) = \overline{A}\,\overline{B}CD + \overline{A}\,\overline{B}C\overline{D} \implies m_3 + m_2 \\
    \overline{A}BD &amp; = \overline{A}BD(C + \overline{C}) = \overline{A}BCD + \overline{A}B\overline{C}D \implies m_7 + m_5 \\
    A\overline{B}\,\overline{C} &amp; = A\overline{B}\,\overline{C}(D + \overline{D}) = A\overline{B}\,\overline{C}D + A\overline{B}\,\overline{C}\,\overline{D} \implies m_9 + m_8 \\
    AB\overline{C}\,\overline{D} &amp; = m_{12}
\end{aligned}\] Combining all the minterms, we get: \[F = \sum m(2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9, 12)\] \[\boxed{\text{(D) } F = \sum m(2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9, 12)}\]

